WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.245
FastAPI applications depend on Python
packages, validation libraries,

2
00:00:08.325 --> 00:00:14.821
authentication libraries, database
drivers, ASGI servers,

3
00:00:14.901 --> 00:00:22.896
container images, operating system
packages, and deployment components.

4
00:00:22.976 --> 00:00:30.096
Dependency hygiene means knowing what is
running, where it is used,

5
00:00:30.176 --> 00:00:38.421
how it is updated, and what risk an
advisory creates in the actual application.

6
00:00:38.501 --> 00:00:43.373
Vulnerability scanning, dependency review,

7
00:00:43.453 --> 00:00:47.700
and secrets scanning support that work,

8
00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:52.777
but findings still need engineering
judgment.

9
00:00:52.857 --> 00:01:00.477
Security-focused tests should cover both
allowed and denied behavior.

10
00:01:00.557 --> 00:01:06.803
Unit and integration tests can verify
request validation,

11
00:01:06.883 --> 00:01:15.503
token validation, authentication
requirements, authorization dependencies,

12
00:01:15.583 --> 00:01:24.202
tenant boundaries, CORS expectations,
response filtering, file upload limits,

13
00:01:24.282 --> 00:01:26.406
and error behavior.

14
00:01:26.486 --> 00:01:34.231
Negative tests are especially valuable
because they prove that invalid,

15
00:01:34.311 --> 00:01:43.555
unauthorized, or out-of-policy requests
fail safely instead of drifting into success

16
00:01:43.635 --> 00:01:44.384
paths.

17
00:01:44.464 --> 00:01:52.709
Logging should help operators investigate
without exposing sensitive data.

18
00:01:52.789 --> 00:02:00.784
API logs can easily capture tokens,
request bodies, headers, identifiers,

19
00:02:00.864 --> 00:02:06.735
regulated data, or secrets if teams are
not deliberate.

20
00:02:06.815 --> 00:02:15.560
Safe logs focus on useful events and
metadata while minimizing sensitive content.

21
00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:21.636
Audit events may include login changes,
token failures,

22
00:02:21.716 --> 00:02:27.712
permission changes, administrative
actions, exports,

23
00:02:27.792 --> 00:02:37.161
unusual denial patterns, configuration
changes, and high-risk workflow transitions.

24
00:02:37.241 --> 00:02:45.486
Monitoring and incident readiness keep API
security alive after deployment.

25
00:02:45.566 --> 00:02:54.310
Rate limiting and throttling concepts can
reduce abuse and protect availability,

26
00:02:54.390 --> 00:03:03.259
but they should complement validation and
authorization rather than replace them.

27
00:03:03.339 --> 00:03:11.584
Teams should know which unusual API
activity matters, how alerts are triaged,

28
00:03:11.664 --> 00:03:14.912
and how to investigate safely.

29
00:03:14.992 --> 00:03:23.237
FastAPI security continues as dependencies
change, traffic patterns shift,

30
00:03:23.317 --> 00:03:25.940
and new routes are added.
